WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.386 --> 00:00:13.017
The first is cool, the second is great, and
the third one is simply incredible to the

00:00:13.017 --> 00:00:18.915
point that I couldn’t believe the results
and had to look over and over again.

00:00:18.915 --> 00:00:21.925
And the fourth is a thing of beauty.

00:00:21.925 --> 00:00:24.415
So, what are the tricks?

00:00:24.415 --> 00:00:29.766
First, this work is called GigaGAN, and it
can perform text to image.

00:00:29.766 --> 00:00:36.056
We Fellow Scholars have seen this before many-many
times, you enter a text prompt and it paints

00:00:36.056 --> 00:00:37.056
you an image.

00:00:37.056 --> 00:00:42.586
What is great about it is that it can give
us reasonably high quality images, that is

00:00:42.586 --> 00:00:49.226
okay, but here is the kicker: it can perform
this in a fraction of a second.

00:00:49.226 --> 00:00:51.185
That is extremely quick.

00:00:51.185 --> 00:00:56.556
For instance, a previous, StyleGAN-based method
that could be roughly as fast, we needed to

00:00:56.556 --> 00:01:00.865
make significant concessions in terms of quality.

00:01:00.865 --> 00:01:02.276
Not anymore.

00:01:02.276 --> 00:01:03.276
Loving it.

00:01:03.276 --> 00:01:10.346
Two, it is not only fast, but it it so fast
that it can create several images per second,

00:01:10.346 --> 00:01:13.866
and thus it offers a controllable latent space.

00:01:13.866 --> 00:01:19.956
This is a hallmark for GAN-based methods and
leads to incredible artistic controllability

00:01:19.956 --> 00:01:21.556
as you see here.

00:01:21.556 --> 00:01:23.576
But, what does all this that mean?

00:01:23.576 --> 00:01:30.406
A GAN-based method means a generative adversarial
network where to neural networks battle each

00:01:30.406 --> 00:01:31.406
other.

00:01:31.406 --> 00:01:36.756
One tries to generate new images to fool the
other one, while this other one trains to

00:01:36.756 --> 00:01:39.976
be able to spot these synthetic images.

00:01:39.976 --> 00:01:44.436
Over time, they battle each other, and they
improve together.

00:01:44.436 --> 00:01:48.616
Now, third, and this is where things get out
of hand.

00:01:48.616 --> 00:01:52.715
Super resolution, or in other words, image
upscaling.

00:01:52.715 --> 00:02:00.275
Here, a coarse image goes in, and the AI guesses
what this image could be, and synthesizes

00:02:00.275 --> 00:02:02.696
a new, really detailed image.

00:02:02.696 --> 00:02:07.086
Now hold on to your papers, and look at that!

00:02:07.086 --> 00:02:13.726
The difference can be really huge: up to a
1000 times more pixels in the new image.

00:02:13.726 --> 00:02:19.726
So how does it compare to previous methods,
for instance, the amazing Stable Diffusion?

00:02:19.726 --> 00:02:22.726
Oh my, look at that.

00:02:22.726 --> 00:02:27.066
It is so much better across the board, pretty
much everywhere.

00:02:27.066 --> 00:02:32.896
But the eyes, the eyes are truly something
else with the new technique.

00:02:32.896 --> 00:02:34.726
What a time to be alive!

00:02:34.726 --> 00:02:36.446
And finally, fourth.

00:02:36.446 --> 00:02:40.586
It also offers a disentangled latent space
for controllability.

00:02:40.586 --> 00:02:45.486
Now, I hear you asking, Doctor, what does
that mean?

00:02:45.486 --> 00:02:51.146
This means that we can control the style in
place with text prompts.

00:02:51.146 --> 00:02:58.545
So whenever we get a teddy bear that we really
like, but we would really like to see it crocheted,

00:02:58.545 --> 00:03:05.155
a bit fluffier, or made of denim, we can do
that, but, without generating a new teddy

00:03:05.155 --> 00:03:06.155
bear.

00:03:06.155 --> 00:03:09.625
All the changes are applied to the same subject.

00:03:09.625 --> 00:03:10.825
That is incredible.

00:03:10.825 --> 00:03:17.226
I also loved the ball example here, this almost
looks like an image straight out of a material

00:03:17.226 --> 00:03:21.366
modeling paper in computer graphics.

00:03:21.366 --> 00:03:26.696
What I absolutely love about this paper is
that normally, for these four problems, we

00:03:26.696 --> 00:03:29.206
would need four separate tools.

00:03:29.206 --> 00:03:30.636
Not anymore!

00:03:30.636 --> 00:03:36.545
Today we are getting all of these amazing
capabilities with just one tool that can do

00:03:36.545 --> 00:03:42.866
all of them, and not only that, but it is
so much faster than previous methods while

00:03:42.866 --> 00:03:46.076
it is still competitive in terms of visual
quality.

00:03:46.076 --> 00:03:52.556
It is not the best and the fastest at the
same time, not even close, but this tradeoff

00:03:52.556 --> 00:03:55.875
is I think an amazing value proposition.

00:03:55.875 --> 00:04:01.376
If you enjoyed this paper, make sure to subscribe
and hit the bell icon to not miss out, we

00:04:01.376 --> 00:04:11.246
have some more amazing papers coming up soon.

00:04:11.246 --> 00:04:49.466
Thanks for watching and 
for your generous support, and I'll see 

00:04:49.466 --> 00:04:51.966
you next time!

